# FPGA bitstreams are binary build artifacts (~2-18 MB each). Marking them
# binary prevents any line-ending or diff processing, which would corrupt a
# .bit file on a Windows checkout.
*.bit binary

# RTL and build inputs stay LF in the working tree on every platform. Git
# otherwise checks these out as CRLF on Windows, which changes their SHA-256
# and made the provenance RTL digest report a false "RTL changed" on every
# Windows clone (verified 2026-07-31). provenance.py normalises line endings
# when hashing as well, so the check is correct either way; this keeps the
# files themselves consistent.
*.v    text eol=lf
*.vh   text eol=lf
*.xdc  text eol=lf
*.tcl  text eol=lf
